Customisations applied:
  - Re-authored the agent-style upstream ("You are a specialist in CoreDNS", "How to Approach
    Tasks" imperative lists) into vault vendor-skill shape: keyword-rich CSO description with
    boundary callout; four-field frontmatter (name, description, license MIT, metadata.version);
    skill marker in SKILL.md only; no frontmatter or skill marker in references.
  - References carry no frontmatter and no skill marker; line 1 of each reference is the # Title.
  - Created references/diagnostics.md as a distinct file (source material had diagnostics
    scattered across SKILL.md "Common Pitfalls" and "How to Approach Tasks" sections); consolidated
    and extended into a structured diagnostics reference with dig patterns, kubectl log workflows,
    plugin debug, per-failure-mode investigation steps, and Prometheus triage table.
  - Dropped "CoreDNS 1.13 Features" and "Custom Go Plugin Development" sections from source: the
    version-specific feature list ages quickly and custom plugin development is out of scope for
    an operator-facing skill; operator flags (DoH3, rate limiting improvements) are noted in
    context where relevant.
  - Em-dash (U+2014) and en-dash (U+2013) glyphs: zero occurrences. ASCII "->" used for
    resolution-flow and architecture arrows; commas, semicolons, and parentheses for pauses.
  - British/Pacific English applied: behaviour, optimise, stabilise, licence (noun).
  - Cross-references scoped to existing vault skills only: dns-network-ops, unbound-dns-ops,
    multi-vendor-network-ops, secrets-hygiene, utc-timestamps, systematic-debugging. No
    kubernetes vault skill exists yet; Kubernetes context is described inline.
  - "When not to use" boundary callouts route architecture/DNSSEC design to dns-network-ops and
    validating-recursive-resolver work to unbound-dns-ops.

Boundary notes:
  coredns-ops = CoreDNS IMPLEMENTATION including Kubernetes cluster DNS, plugin chain
  configuration, NodeLocal DNSCache, Corefile authoring, and CoreDNS-specific diagnostics.
  dns-network-ops owns DNS architecture, DNSSEC design, cross-platform comparison, and the
  conceptual layer (resolution flow, record types, zone transfer, caching strategy).
  unbound-dns-ops owns validating caching recursive resolver implementation.
  Kubernetes DNS concepts (service discovery model, search domains, ndots) are covered inline
  in coredns-ops because CoreDNS is the implementation vehicle; the broader Kubernetes platform
  is out of scope for the DNS family.

